  q: "A man sustains a blow to the forehead and loses consciousness. At autopsy, a contusion is found beneath the site of impact, with another contusion on the opposite side of the brain. Which mechanism best explains the contralateral lesion?",
  opts: ["Contrecoup injury caused by brain movement and deformation within the skull", "An abrasion caused by the weapon", "A primary spinal cord injury", "A postmortem artifact caused by rigor mortis"],
  ans: 0,
  exp: "Contrecoup injury occurs when trauma causes brain movement and deformation that produce injury opposite the site of impact. Coup injuries occur beneath or near the impact site, while contrecoup injuries may occur contralaterally. Actual injury distribution depends on impact mechanics, head motion, and anatomy.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option B (Abrasion):</strong> An abrasion is a superficial skin injury and does not explain a contralateral intracranial contusion.<br>• <strong>Option C (Spinal cord injury):</strong> A spinal cord lesion does not explain the described cerebral contusions.<br>• <strong>Option D (Rigor mortis artifact):</strong> Rigor mortis affects skeletal muscles and does not produce this characteristic traumatic brain injury pattern."
