  subject: "Forensic Medicine",
  topic: "Toxicology",
  chapter: "Atropine in Organophosphorus Poisoning",
  level: "Moderate",
  q: "A patient with organophosphorus pesticide poisoning has received atropine. The patient remains severely hypoxic with copious bronchial secretions. Which endpoint is most important when titrating atropine?",
  opts: ["Complete disappearance of skeletal muscle fasciculations", "Adequate oxygenation and ventilation with control of bronchial secretions and improvement in hemodynamic status", "Normalization of pupil size alone", "Immediate normalization of plasma cholinesterase activity"],
  ans: 1,
  exp: "Atropine competitively antagonizes muscarinic acetylcholine receptors. In organophosphorus poisoning, it is titrated to clinical endpoints, particularly improved oxygenation, drying of bronchial secretions, and adequate circulation. It does not directly reverse nicotinic weakness, and cholinesterase activity does not need to normalize for clinical improvement.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Disappearance of fasciculations):</strong> Fasciculations reflect nicotinic effects and are not the principal atropine endpoint.<br>• <strong>Option C (Pupil normalization):</strong> Pupil size alone is an unreliable guide to adequate atropinization.<br>• <strong>Option D (Immediate cholinesterase normalization):</strong> Atropine blocks muscarinic receptors but does not reactivate acetylcholinesterase."
