  topic: "Toxicology",
  chapter: "Methanol Poisoning",
  level: "Hard",
  q: "A man presents after consuming illicitly distilled alcohol. He develops severe metabolic acidosis, visual blurring, and reduced consciousness. Which toxic metabolite is primarily responsible for the characteristic visual toxicity?",
  opts: ["Acetaldehyde", "Formic acid", "Acetone", "Oxalic acid"],
  ans: 1,
  exp: "Methanol is metabolized by alcohol dehydrogenase to formaldehyde and then to formic acid. Formate contributes to metabolic acidosis and mitochondrial dysfunction, particularly in the retina and optic nerve, producing visual disturbances that may progress to blindness. Management includes antidotal inhibition of alcohol dehydrogenase, correction of acidosis, and dialysis when indicated.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Acetaldehyde):</strong> Acetaldehyde is the principal intermediate in ethanol metabolism, not the characteristic toxic metabolite of methanol.<br>• <strong>Option C (Acetone):</strong> Acetone may be elevated in selected metabolic states but does not explain the classic formate-mediated visual toxicity.<br>• <strong>Option D (Oxalic acid):</strong> Oxalate is associated with ethylene glycol metabolism and calcium oxalate deposition."
